## 5. Component Design

### 5.1 Upload Path — API Gateway + S3 Presigned URL

- User calls `POST /request-upload` via API Gateway HTTP API.
- Lambda generates an S3 **presigned multipart upload URL** (valid 60 min).
- Browser uploads directly to S3 in parallel parts — bypasses API Gateway's 10 MB and Lambda's 6 MB limits entirely.
- On upload complete, S3 emits an event to EventBridge.

### 5.2 Event Routing — EventBridge

- S3 `ObjectCreated` events are forwarded to EventBridge.
- EventBridge rule matches on the uploads bucket prefix and triggers Step Functions.
- Decoupled by design — future consumers (audit log, analytics) can subscribe without touching the upload path.

### 5.3 Orchestration — Step Functions Standard Workflow

```
[Start]
   │
   ▼
[Scrub Task]  ──fail──► [Catch → Update DDB → SNS Error]
   │
   ▼
[Enrich Task] ──fail──► [Catch → Update DDB → SNS Error]
   │
   ▼
[Load Task]   ──fail──► [Catch → Update DDB → SNS Error]
   │
   ▼
[Update DDB: COMPLETE]
   │
   ▼
[SNS: Notify User Success]
   │
[End]
```

- Per-stage retry with exponential backoff.
- Full execution history visible in AWS Console.
- Runs well beyond the 15-minute Lambda ceiling.

### 5.4 Compute — ECS Fargate (3 Task Definitions)

| Task | Responsibility | IAM Scope |
|---|---|---|
| scrub | Read from uploads bucket, remove PII fields, write to processed bucket | Read uploads prefix, write processed prefix |
| enrich | Read processed, call external API, write enriched output | Read processed prefix, write enriched prefix, NAT egress |
| load | Read enriched, bulk COPY INTO RDS Postgres | Read enriched prefix, RDS write |

- No 15-min Lambda ceiling.
- Real filesystem for large file manipulation.
- No EKS overhead.

### 5.5 Job State — DynamoDB

- Schema: `jobId (PK)` → `{ status, fileName, startedAt, updatedAt, error }`
- Step Functions writes status at each stage transition.
- `GET /get-status?jobId=xxx` Lambda reads directly — single-digit ms latency.

### 5.6 Final Sink — RDS Postgres Multi-AZ

- Bulk load via `COPY FROM` for fast ingestion.
- Multi-AZ for automatic failover (< 2 min RTO).
- Credentials stored in Secrets Manager, injected as ECS task secrets.

### 5.7 Notifications — SNS

- Two topics: `user-notifications` and `ops-alerts`.
- User topic: email on success/failure.
- Ops topic: PagerDuty/Slack on failure.

### 5.8 Status API — API Gateway + Lambda

- `POST /request-upload` — returns presigned URL + jobId.
- `GET /get-status?jobId=xxx` — returns current job status from DynamoDB.
- HTTP API (~70% cheaper than REST API).

---

## 6. Data Flow

```
Step 1 — Upload
  Browser calls /request-upload
  → Lambda creates DDB record (status: PENDING)
  → Lambda returns presigned multipart URL + jobId
  → Browser uploads CSV directly to S3 (uploads bucket)

Step 2 — Trigger
  S3 ObjectCreated event
  → EventBridge rule matches
  → Step Functions execution starts
  → DDB updated (status: PROCESSING)

Step 3 — Scrub
  Fargate Task 1 reads raw CSV from uploads bucket
  → Removes PII fields
  → Writes clean file to processed bucket
  → DDB updated (status: SCRUBBED)

Step 4 — Enrich
  Fargate Task 2 reads from processed bucket
  → Calls external enrichment API (via NAT Gateway)
  → Writes enriched file to processed bucket
  → DDB updated (status: ENRICHED)

Step 5 — Load
  Fargate Task 3 reads enriched file
  → COPY INTO RDS Postgres
  → DDB updated (status: COMPLETE)
  → SNS notification sent to user

On any failure:
  Step Functions Catch block fires
  → DDB updated (status: FAILED, error: <reason>)
  → SNS ops-alert sent
```

---

## 7. Decision Log

| Component | Choice | Reason | Alternative Rejected |
|---|---|---|---|
| Region | eu-north-1 Stockholm | Co-located with company & users; GDPR-default; ~10–15% cheaper; 100% renewable energy | Frankfurt — more expensive, no business reason |
| Upload path | S3 presigned multipart PUT direct from browser | API GW has 10 MB limit; Lambda has 6 MB sync limit. Direct to S3 bypasses both, supports parallel parts up to 5 GB | Upload through API Gateway — impossible at 5 GB |
| Trigger | S3 → EventBridge → Step Functions | Decoupled; supports fan-out to future consumers without touching upload path | S3 → Lambda direct — tighter coupling, harder to extend |
| Orchestrator | Step Functions Standard | Per-stage retry/catch, visible execution history, native integrations, no 5-min ceiling | Express SFN — 5-min limit; raw ECS — no retry or state machine |
| Compute | ECS Fargate (3 task definitions) | No 15-min Lambda ceiling; real filesystem; no EKS overhead; ~$0.02–0.05/file | Lambda — too close to limits; Batch — unnecessary scheduler hop; Glue — not designed for rate-limited REST API enrichment |
| Job state | DynamoDB | Single-digit ms reads for status polling; simple key-value lookups | RDS — wastes connection pool on operational data |
| Final sink | RDS Postgres Multi-AZ | Prompt specifies "main database"; COPY FROM for fast bulk load | Aurora Serverless v2 — good alternative if writes are bursty |
| HA | Multi-AZ RDS + 2-AZ private subnets | 3 AZs available in Stockholm; Multi-AZ covers DB failover | Multi-region — unnecessary overhead for Stockholm-only company |
| Encryption | KMS CMK end-to-end | PII compliance; covers S3, RDS, DDB, SNS, Secrets Manager, CloudWatch logs | SSE-S3 — no customer-managed key control |
| Secrets | Secrets Manager | Auto-rotation capable; injected as ECS task secrets, not environment variables | SSM Parameter Store — works but Secrets Manager is the correct semantic for credentials |
| API | API Gateway HTTP API + 2 Lambdas | HTTP API is ~70% cheaper than REST API; Lambda is the right size for lightweight JSON endpoints | API GW REST API — unnecessary overhead |
| Notifications | SNS (user + ops topics) | Cheap, durable, fans out to email/Slack/PagerDuty without code changes | SES direct — too narrow, single delivery channel |
| DR | Cross-region S3 replication to eu-central-1 (Frankfurt), STANDARD_IA | Low-cost insurance (~$0.02/GB) for backup recovery | Aurora Global DB — expensive without a defined RTO requirement |
| Networking | VPC with public + 2 private subnets, NAT for enrich egress, VPC Endpoints | Fargate-to-AWS-service traffic stays off NAT; only enrich-to-external-API uses NAT | Public Fargate — security regression for a PII workload |

---

## 8. Trade-offs

| Decision | Accepted Trade-off | Risk Level | When to Revisit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Single NAT Gateway (not one per AZ) | Single point of failure for enrich egress only | Low | If enrich task SLA becomes critical |
| No CloudFront / Transfer Acceleration | Slightly higher latency for non-Stockholm users | Low | If user base expands beyond Stockholm |
| No WAF on API Gateway | API exposed without request filtering or rate limiting | Medium | Before public production launch |
| No Cognito / API auth in Terraform | Endpoints are unauthenticated in current design | High | Must be resolved before production |
| `force_destroy = true` on S3 buckets | Risk of accidental data loss in staging | Medium | Set to `false` for production |
| `skip_final_snapshot = true` on RDS | No automated snapshot on destroy | Medium | Set to `false` for production |

---

## 9. GDPR / PII Posture

| Control | Implementation |
|---|---|
| Public access | All buckets: `BlockPublicAcls = true` + deny-non-TLS bucket policy |
| Encryption | KMS CMK on uploads bucket, processed bucket, RDS, DynamoDB, SNS, CloudWatch Logs |
| PII retention | Raw uploads bucket: 1-day lifecycle expiry — PII is not retained beyond processing |
| Least privilege | Per-stage IAM roles scope each Fargate task to only its required S3 prefix |
| Audit trail | CloudTrail data events enabled on uploads bucket |
| Secrets | DB password + external API key stored in Secrets Manager; injected as ECS task secrets, never as environment variables |
| Data residency | All data remains in eu-north-1; S3 DR replication to eu-central-1 for backup only |

> ⚠️ **Schrems II Flag:** If the enrichment vendor is US-based, the scrub task **must** remove all PII fields before the enrich task runs. This is a vendor contract and data processing agreement concern, not an infrastructure one — but the pipeline sequence enforces the right order.

---

## 10. Deployment & CI/CD

- All infrastructure managed via **Terraform**.
- Deployed via **AWS CodeBuild** pipeline on merge to `main`.
- Build steps: `terraform init` → `terraform plan` → `terraform apply`.
- Terraform state stored in **S3 backend** with versioning enabled.
- State locking via **DynamoDB lock table** to prevent concurrent applies.

```
GitHub / CodeCommit (main branch)
        │
        ▼ merge
   AWS CodeBuild
        │
        ├── terraform init
        ├── terraform plan
        └── terraform apply
               │
               ▼
        AWS Infrastructure (eu-north-1)
```

---

## 11. Open Items & Future Work

| Item | Priority | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Add Cognito / Lambda authorizer to API Gateway | High | Required before production launch |
| Add WAF to API Gateway | High | Rate limiting + request filtering |
| Set `force_destroy = false` and `skip_final_snapshot = false` | High | Before production deployment |
| Define concurrent upload limit and test at scale | Medium | Validate Fargate task concurrency limits |
| Enrichment vendor Schrems II review | Medium | Legal + security sign-off required |
| Add CloudFront if users expand globally | Low | Not needed for Stockholm-only users |
| Consider Aurora Serverless v2 if write patterns become bursty | Low | Monitor RDS metrics post-launch |
| One NAT per AZ for enrich HA | Low | Revisit if enrich SLA tightens |

